# ClaudeMdInjector backup (session artifact, never commit)
.CLAUDE.md.pre-inject

# Plan-local script output (triage/acceptance/deploy reports).
# The LOGS live in the plan folder so they travel with the plan (see
# CLAUDE/PlanWorkflow.md "Plan-Local Scripts & Artifacts"), but they are NOT
# committed: they are dumps of live host state — unit files, mount points,
# container names, private IPs, SSIDs — and this is a public repository. The
# pre-commit secret scanner would reject most of them anyway.
# KEPT DELIBERATELY, after Plan 00130 asked whether it should go. It is what made
# three orphaned logs/ dirs invisible to every `git status` that would have shown
# them — but the visibility gap is now closed by scripts/qa-plan-script-logging.bash,
# which rejects a plan-local logs/ dir by name. That leaves this entry doing the one
# job the gate cannot: a gate can be skipped, and if it is, an unscrubbed dump of live
# host state must still not be committable to a public repo. Defence in depth with the
# blind spot closed, rather than a single control that fails open.
CLAUDE/Plan/**/logs/

# Scratch space for raw captures: journal excerpts, process dumps, triage working
# notes, agent reports. Same reasoning as the plan logs above — live host state in
# a public repository.
#
# Belt and braces, deliberately. untracked/.gitignore already self-excludes the
# directory ("*" plus "!.gitignore", so the rule itself stays tracked and ships
# with the repo), which means this line is redundant — and that is the point. Two
# independent rules means deleting either one still leaves the directory
# protected, and neither depends on the developer's user-global gitignore, which
# covers this path locally but ships with nothing.
#
# Written as "untracked/*" and not "/untracked/" so the nested .gitignore can be
# re-included: git does not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a negation
# inside one can never take effect. Excluding the directory's contents instead
# leaves the negation reachable.
untracked/*
!untracked/.gitignore

# The sensitive_content handler's word list. Its whole purpose is to name things
# that must never leave this machine, so the list itself must never be committed.
*.secrets
